  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAnother quiet morning across the area amid clear skies courtesy\nof lingering surface high pressure. Temperatures this morning\nare generally 10 degrees warmer than that of yesterday. Patchy\nriver valley fog is developing in spots, primarily south of the\nOhio River, but coverage is much less widespread than that of\nyesterday morning.\n\nHeat and humidity will continue to increase across the area\ntoday as southwest to west low-level flow ushers in an \nincreasingly humid Gulf airmass around the periphery of an \nupper ridge centered near the Tennessee Valley. Highs today and\ntomorrow are progged to be the warmest of the week, with upper \n80s to low 90s expected across much of the lowlands. The \ncombination of the aforementioned temperatures with dew points \nin the low 70s, will result in heat indices during the \nafternoons reaching the mid 90s to 100 across the lowlands. \nOverall, think today will be the hottest day, with increased\ncloud cover and the chance for showers/storms on Thursday \npotentially providing a bit of relief. Be sure to take frequent\nbreaks if outside during peak heating hours the next couple of \ndays.\n\nSome relief is expected on Friday as the aforementioned frontal\nboundary slides south of the area, bringing a return to near \nseasonable values - low/mid 80s across the lowlands. Warmer and \nhumid conditions, albeit not as bad as today and Thursday, \nreturn over the weekend as the front buckles back north of the \nCWA. Highs are progged for the mid/upper 80s across the lowlands.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nAfter a brief quiet stretch, active weather quickly returns to \nthe area later today, likely persisting through the weekend and\ninto at least early next week. A cold front currently \npositioned across the northern Ohio Valley will gradually move \nsouthward towards the area throughout today. The front is \nprogged to become quasi-stationary across the general Ohio \nValley region tonight through the weekend, meandering \nnorth/south at times. Current thinking is that the front slips \nsouth of the area on Friday, then buckles back north during the \nday on Saturday, then lingers across the northern Ohio Valley \ninto early next week, before eventually clearing east/south \ntowards mid next week courtesy of a more robust upper trough \n(confidence this far out remains low).\n\nThis will result in daily chances for showers and storms for the\nforeseeable future, with rain chances being highly correlated \nto the position of the frontal boundary. Given the overall \nrecent wet pattern we've been in, and the return of a very moist\nairmass with progged PWATs pushing 1.7-2.1\" at times, the \nconcern for flash flooding will return, especially given the \npotential for west-east training thunderstorms along the front \n(given upper-level flow being parallel to the front). The period\nof greatest concern in the near term is tonight and during the \nday on Thursday. WPC has introduced a Marginal to Slight Risk \nfor excessive rainfall across northwest portions of the CWA for \ntonight, while a Marginal Risk for much of the area on Thursday.\nWhile confidence is not high enough for a Flood Watch at this \ntime, this will be monitored going forward, with a targeted \nwatch not out of the question if confidence in training storms \nacross a particular area were to increase. A general reprieve is\nexpected on Friday, with increasing shower/storm chances \nSaturday into Sunday as the front lifts back north across the \narea.\n\nThe other aspect of this pattern will be the potential for\nstrong to isolated severe thunderstorms at times, once again\nhinging on the position of the frontal boundary. The general \nsetup will feature modest shear (15-25 kts / 0-6km) and modest \nbuoyancy (750-1,500 Mixed-layer CAPE) amid rather saturated \nprofiles. This translates into a strong to isolated damaging \nwind threat with stronger (diurnally driven) collapsing cores \nvia wet downbursts. The first potential for this is expected \nlate this afternoon into early tonight across the Mid-Ohio \nValley, with SPC having introduced a Marginal Risk for severe \nthunderstorms there. Some potential will also exist tomorrow \nafternoon/evening, albeit probably further south in the CWA. A \nreprieve is expected on Friday.",
